+## R11. The §3.2 refusal hook is bypassable by `--no-verify`; even un-bypassed it refuses the *symptom*, not the drift. — maps to §3.2, §9 P3; prediction **P3**. **STANDS (new; explicit).**
+
+The sponsor accepted O7 and adopted a canonical-checkout `pre-commit` refusal
+hook as a "primary control" (§3.2; §11), while *acknowledging* that `--no-verify`
+can bypass it (§3.2, line 132). Acknowledging a defect and retaining the
+defective control is not a resolution:
+
+- **Trivially bypassable.** `git commit --no-verify` (alias `-n`) skips the
+  `pre-commit` and `commit-msg` hooks. An agent that has drifted the checkout
+  onto a ballot branch and still wishes to commit there bypasses the refusal
+  with one flag. The "refusal" is a speed bump, not a control.
+- **Wrong event.** The drift is the checkout **leaving `main`** (`git switch -c
+  <branch>`), which a `pre-commit` hook never observes; the hook fires only on
+  the later `git commit`. So even a non-bypassed hook cannot prevent the
+  canonical checkout from drifting — it can only refuse one commit. This is why
+  P3 ("zero seat/office commits on a non-`main` branch") can pass while drift
+  continues unabated (R4.1).
+- **Still no record.** A refused or bypassed commit writes nothing to
+  `records/canonical-drift/` and emits no signal (R4.2), so the hook serves
+  neither prevention nor audit.
+
+The durable, unbypassable control is a `post-checkout`/`reference-transaction`
+hook or a filesystem watch at the drift event itself (A3′), not a `pre-commit`
+refusal that `--no-verify` defeats.
+
+## R12. Fast-forward impact (O8): verified resolved in v2 §1; the residual within-cycle hazard is an S6-restore dependency already covered by R1/R3. — maps to §1, §4; prediction **P2**. **RESOLVED (no residual false claim).**
+
+The prior red-team O8 (§6) held that §1's "`git pull --ff-only` ... can pull into
+a ballot branch" hazard was stale. I re-verified the S6 code: the switch to
+`main` (`constitution/boot.sh:33-35`) precedes `fetch origin main` +
+`merge --ff-only FETCH_HEAD` (`:38-39`), and `git pull` is never used. The
+sponsor accepted O8 (§7.8) and amended §1 to state the impact as the
+**within-cycle misrouting of a concurrent commit** (§1, lines 63-66). That
+restatement is accurate. **No residual false claim.**
+
+The residual concern is not the fast-forward description but the *dependence*:
+the within-cycle hazard persists precisely because the only backstop is the S6
+restore, which runs once per cycle and is silent (R1). If that restore fails —
+e.g. a dirty-tree switch refusal (O6) or any non-zero return before the fetch —
+the checkout stays on the drift branch until the next cycle, and the concurrent
+commit is misrouted. This is the same S6-owned control that R3 says must not
+trigger repeal. So O8 is closed; the surviving defect is the one R1/R3 name.
+
